<div id=Question>
Can you solve the following math problem?<br>Candice put <strong>80</strong> post-it notes in her purse before she headed out to her job at the coffee shop. On her way, she stopped off at the store and purchased a package of Post-it notes; At work, she placed a single Post-it note on each of <strong>220</strong> different cups of coffee. If she had <strong>23</strong> post-it notes remaining overall, how many Post-it notes were in the package that she purchased? Explain your reasoning.
</div>

<div id=Working>
First, let's figure out how many Post-it notes Candice used at work: <br>
Candice placed <strong>220</strong> notes on cups of coffee. At the beginning, she had <strong>80</strong> notes in her purse, so she used <strong>80 + 220 = 300</strong> notes in total.<br><br>
Next, we know that she had <strong>23</strong> Post-it notes left in her purse, so the total number of notes she purchased (including the ones she initially had) is: <strong>300 + 23 = 323</strong>.<br><br>
The rest of the notes she purchased were in the package she bought. So, the number of Post-it notes in the package is: <strong>323 - 80 = 243</strong>.
</div>

<div id=Answer>
\boxed{\textbf{Answer:} 243}
</div>

Solution:
First, add the number of Post-it notes Candice initially had (80) and the number she used on cups of coffee (220), to get the total number of Post-it notes she used (300). Then, subtract the number of Post-it notes Candice had at the end (23) to find out the sum of the Post-it notes in the package she purchased (243).

Final answer: \boxed{243}

